From 5cb19911e7ce6b01e6bab97843a4056fd1a20a78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 11:31:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] ifreload: add applet to reconcile interface state with
 config
Organization: Wires

ifupdown-ng brings interfaces up and down, but has no reload: ifup -a
only brings interfaces up and never tears down ones dropped from the
config.  ifreload keeps a copy of the last-applied config and, on each
run, diffs it against the current config -- bringing up new interfaces,
tearing down removed ones (using the old config so the right down
executors run), re-applying changed ones, and leaving unchanged
interfaces alone so a reload never bounces what did not change.

The last-applied copy lives on tmpfs (/run/ifreload), so a fresh boot
has none and the first run simply records the baseline.

Signed-off-by: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
---
